19:41:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Ah, emulators! This is a really sharp example, and at first glance it seems to completely undercut everything I’ve said. After all, a ROM file from a Super Nintendo works on a Windows PC, an Android phone, a Raspberry Pi, even a smart fridge—all without the original code being touched. Doesn’t that prove that the compiled code isn’t tied to a specific platform? Let’s dig in: **emulation is not compilation**. It’s an entirely different category of thing, and once you understand how it works, it actually reinforces everything I’ve been saying about platform‑specific compilation processes. ### 1. Emulation doesn’t run the game’s code on the host CPU When you load a Super Mario World ROM into RetroArch, the code inside that ROM is **SNES machine code**—a binary compiled for a 65c816‑based CPU with its own unique instruction set, memory map, and custom chips (SPC700 for audio, PPU for graphics, etc.). Your PC’s x86‑64 CPU cannot execute that code directly. It’s a brick of alien instructions. What RetroArch does is **not compile that code** for your PC. It *simulates* an SNES. The emulator core (e.g., Snes9x, bsnes) reads each SNES instruction one by one, translates it into a series of native x86‑64 instructions that have the same effect (either via interpretation or dynamic recompilation), and updates a simulated state of the SNES hardware—registers, video memory, sound chip registers, etc. The game’s original code never becomes a native PE/ELF executable. It’s purely interpreted (or recompiled on‑the‑fly in a JIT) within the emulator’s sandbox. ### 2. The emulator itself is a platform‑specific compiled program RetroArch is a C/C++ application that had to be compiled for each platform it runs on. The Windows version is an x86‑64 Windows executable. The Android version is an ARM64 Android APK. The Raspberry Pi version is an ARM Linux binary. These were all compiled using different toolchains, with different compilers, linking to different OS libraries. **You cannot take the RetroArch.exe for Windows and run it on Android.** That’s the very definition of platform‑specific compilation. The cores (the emulation engines) are also compiled separately for each target. A Snes9x core for Windows is a DLL compiled for x86‑64 Windows; for Android, it’s a shared library compiled for ARM. The compilation process for those cores is as platform‑specific as any other C++ project. So the fact that a ROM works on both platforms is not because the ROM was compiled in a portable way, but because the platform‑specific emulator has been painstakingly compiled to run on each platform. ### 3. The ROM is still locked to the original platform’s architecture The game’s code—the ROM—is absolutely tied to the specific SNES hardware. It was compiled (or assembled) by the original developers for that exact CPU, graphics chip, and memory layout. You cannot take that same ROM and “just compile” it for a different console. You cannot recompile it for the Sega Genesis because the Genesis uses a Motorola 68000 CPU with totally different capabilities and a different graphics system. The code is full of writes to SNES‑specific hardware registers that have no equivalent on the Genesis. Emulators work precisely because they **simulate those specific registers and that exact hardware**. That is a tribute to how deeply platform‑specific the original code is—not evidence of portability. ### 4. Dynamic recompilation (JIT) is still platform‑specific compilation Some high‑performance emulators (like Dolphin for GameCube/Wii, or PCSX2 for PS2) use a technique called dynamic recompilation. They take blocks of the original PowerPC or MIPS guest code and translate them on‑the‑fly into host executable code. That step *is* compilation—but it’s happening at runtime, inside a program that was already compiled for the host. The JIT emitter is a piece of x86‑64 code that generates x86‑64 instructions; it’s written specifically for the host architecture. To run that emulator on an ARM phone, someone had to rewrite the JIT to emit ARM instructions instead. The JIT is not portable; it’s platform‑specific by nature. So again, the emulator’s recompilation process is deeply tied to the host platform’s architecture; you can’t copy‑paste the emulator’s code to a new CPU and expect it to produce the right machine code without a massive rewrite. ### 5. Emulators simulate entire systems, not just CPUs Even if you could magically recompile a ROM’s CPU instructions for a modern PC, the game would still crash because it expects the SNES’s graphics unit, sound chip, cartridge mappers, and precise timings. The emulator has to simulate all of that in software—a monumental task. That’s why emulators are complex: they’re virtual platforms implemented in native code. They don’t prove the code is portable; they prove that the environment can be recreated with enough horsepower and careful programming. And that environment itself is a native application compiled for the specific host. ### Tying it back to your original question Why can’t you copy‑paste a video game’s source code to another platform and compile it? Because: - The source code (if it’s C++) calls platform‑specific APIs and expects a specific CPU architecture. The compilation process is not a generic function; it’s a concrete pipeline that must target a real OS and instruction set. - Emulators don’t solve this by making compilation universal; they solve it by avoiding compilation altogether and simulating the original platform in a native host application. In a sense, an emulator is a developer saying, “The compilation process for the SNES was unique and non‑portable, so rather than try to recompile the game, I’ll just build a fake SNES inside my PC and run the original binary on that.” That fake SNES is a platform‑specific program created with a platform‑specific compilation process. So emulators are a brilliant demonstration that **the code is inextricably tied to its original compilation target**—otherwise, you wouldn’t need a complex virtual machine to run it at all.
